  PARITY (the point of this example)
    ENDF route : k_eff = 1.00276 +/- 0.00071
    ACE  route : k_eff = 1.00168 +/- 0.00099
    difference : -108.1 pcm  (combined sigma 121.7 pcm, 0.89 sigma)
    => the two routes AGREE within statistics.

    The lattice on an 11-nuclide tier: 24 clad and boron trace nuclides are
    dropped, so k is close to, but not exactly, the full model's.

  THE ASYMMETRY, PRICED (GitHub #307 item 5)
    ENDF ablated : k_eff = 1.00441 +/- 0.00046   (DBRC off; URR on, as in route B)
    ablated - ENDF : +164.6 +/- 84.5 pcm (1.95 sigma)          -- the worth of DBRC here
    ACE - ablated  : -272.7 +/- 109.2 pcm (2.50 sigma)          -- the routes with the SAME physics
    ACE - ENDF     : -108.1 +/- 121.7 pcm (0.89 sigma)          -- what was quoted before
    change in gap  : +164.6 +/- 163.6 pcm (1.01 sigma)
    => removing the asymmetry moved the arms FURTHER APART by 164.6 pcm, more
       than the uncertainty on that change, so the -108.1 pcm is not explained by
       URR+DBRC and the routes differ for another reason.
    per-seed spread: ENDF sd 200 pcm, ACE sd 280 pcm, ablated sd 130 pcm over 8 seeds
    NOTE ON PAIRING: URR and DBRC change how many variates a history draws, so
             the ablated arm's random stream DIVERGES from the unablated one. These are
             independent runs, not a paired difference -- the sigmas above are sqrt(2) x the
             per-arm sigma and no variance cancels. Measured in
             verification_and_validation/ace_route_physics/urr_dbrc_worth_2026_09_25.md.

